**Abstract**

This paper develops a comprehensive nowcasting framework for South African quarterly GDP year-over-year (YoY) growth rates using a mixed-frequency dataset spanning 1999Q1 through 2025Q4. The indicator set includes 19 carefully selected variables: 10 monthly economic flows—retail sales, wholesale sales, passenger vehicle sales, private credit extension, electricity consumption and production, building plans passed and completed, mining sales, and M3 money supply—are log-transformed to achieve stationarity and stabilize exponential trends inherent in nominal flow data. Nine additional stationary monthly indices (New Vehicle Sales Index, Business Cycle Composite Leader, CPI All Items, REER, PMI) and quarterly surveys (Business Confidence, Building Confidence, Production Volume, Industrial Production Index) enter in raw levels, preserving their normalized scale and diffusion properties.

The econometric strategy employs a diverse model suite optimized for nowcasting: AR(1,4) autoregressive benchmarks capture GDP persistence; ARMA(1,0,2) specifications with lags determined via systematic ACF/PACF analysis accommodate serial correlation in stationary GDP differences; pooled Indicator (bridge) regressions leverage monthly data for quarterly targets; VAR models incorporate dynamic interrelationships; MIDAS regressions handle mixed frequencies; a 5-factor Dynamic Factor Model extracts common cycles; and LASSO regularization addresses multicollinearity among correlated predictors. A binary dummy variable (=1 from 2016Q1 onward) controls for structural economic shifts observed in the post-global financial crisis period.

Models undergo rigorous step-ahead forecasting validation through recursive estimation: initial training on 1999Q1–2021Q4 generates the 2022Q1 forecast, with subsequent rounds expanding the training sample by one quarter using 4-quarter rolling windows. This recursive approach mimics real-time nowcasting conditions, ensuring vintage data consistency and out-of-sample reliability.

The principal innovation—a forecast combination methodically weighted by inverse mean squared errors (MSE_it) from rolling-window performance—aggregates individual model predictions into a single, robust nowcast. This ensemble approach systematically outperforms constituent models by exploiting complementary strengths and mitigating individual biases, delivering superior real-time tracking of South African GDP growth.

The framework effectively bridges South Africa's characteristic 85-day quarterly GDP publication lag, providing policymakers, analysts, and market participants with timely, high-frequency insights into current economic conditions and near-term growth trajectories.

